{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 38, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a nobleman and leader of the County of Tripoli a crusader state in the Holy Land. He was born in 1140 and was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. *Early Life* Raymond III was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. After the assassination of his father in 1152 Raymond III became the Count of Tripoli at the age of 12 and his mother Hodierna acted as regent until he came of age.\n\nRaymond III was a key figure in the politics of the crusader states in the Holy Land. He was a member of the Haute Cour of Jerusalem and played a significant role in the regency of the Kingdom of Jerusalem during the minority of King Baldwin IV. *Regency in Jerusalem* Raymond III was appointed as regent for King Baldwin IV in 1174 and again in 1176. During his regency Raymond III worked to strengthen the kingdom's defenses and to secure alliances with neighboring states.\n\nIn 1180 Raymond III arranged for the marriage of his stepdaughter Sibylla to Guy of Lusignan a nobleman from Poitou. However this marriage was not popular with all members of the Haute Cour and it contributed to the divisions within the kingdom. *Conflict with Guy of Lusignan* Raymond III was a rival of Guy of Lusignan and the two men had significant differences. In 1187 Raymond III allied himself with Saladin the Ayyubid sultan in an attempt to prevent Guy of Lusignan from becoming king.\n\nRaymond III died in 1187 without an heir and the County of Tripoli passed to his godson Raymond IV Count of Tripoli who was the son of Bohemond III Prince of Antioch. Raymond III's legacy is complex and his actions were motivated by a desire to protect his own interests and those of the County of Tripoli. He played a significant role in the history of the crusader states and his decisions had important consequences for the Kingd